  "requirement": "`habeas mdbook supports html` MUST succeed, and a support query for another renderer MUST fail. An unknown command or invalid setup option combination MUST fail with a usage message. In particular, `habeas agents` and `habeas setup --install-skills` MUST fail with a usage message.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the sources satisfy `requirements.cli.invocation`. The `{impl}` marking, the behavior and the tests all line up with the requirement.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `src/main.rs` `main` has the `{impl}` marker on the `Command::Mdbook { command: Some(MdbookCommand::Supports { renderer }) }` arm.\n  - It returns `Ok(())` when the renderer is `\"html\"`, so the process succeeds.\n  - It calls `std::process::exit(1)` for any other renderer, so the process fails.\n- The workspace-evidence load is skipped for the `mdbook supports` query. A support query therefore does not depend on workspace configuration. That skip carries the configuration `{impl}` markers, not this requirement's marker.\n- Usage failures come from the clap derive (`Cli`, `Command`, `Cli::parse()`), which is already marked `requirements.cli.clap`.\n  - An unknown command such as `agents` or `unknown` makes clap exit nonzero with a usage message.\n  - `Setup` accepts only `--path`. So `--wrong` and `--install-skills`, alone or combined with `--path`, are rejected by clap with usage output. Removed features like `agents` and `--install-skills` therefore fail as the requirement says.\n- I found no behavior in the `{impl}` arm beyond what the requirement specifies.\n\n**Tests (`tests/cli.rs`)**\n- `renderer_support_and_invalid_setup_option` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It checks that:\n  - `mdbook supports html` succeeds;\n  - `mdbook supports epub` fails;\n  - `agents`, `setup --wrong`, `setup --install-skills` and `setup --path . --install-skills` each exit nonzero with stderr containing `Usage: habeas`.\n- `unknown_command_fails_with_usage` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It runs `habeas unknown` and checks for a nonzero exit and a usage message. It is also the test for the example `reference.cli.unknown_command`.\n- Together these cover every explicit obligation: html success, non-html failure, unknown command, the specific `habeas agents` and `habeas setup --install-skills` cases, and an invalid setup option.\n\n**Gaps**\n- I found no gaps and no FIXME comments.",
